-- title:
--   Units of ℤₚ as (1+p)^j zᵖ, p odd
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime with $p \neq 2$, and let $u \in \mathbb{Z}_p$ satisfy $\lVert u \rVert = 1$, i.e. $u$ is a unit of the ring of $p$-adic integers. The conclusion is that there exist a natural number $j < p$ and an element $z \in \mathbb{Z}_p$ with $u = (1 + p)^j \cdot z^p$, where $1 + p$ denotes the image of the natural number $1 + p$ in $\mathbb{Z}_p$. Thus every $p$-adic unit is the product of a power of the principal unit $1 + p$ with exponent in the range $0 \le j < p$ and a $p$-th power in $\mathbb{Z}_p$; no uniqueness of $j$, and no condition on $z$ beyond membership in $\mathbb{Z}_p$ (in particular not that $z$ be a unit), is asserted.
--
--   This is the statement that $\mathbb{Z}_p^\times/(\mathbb{Z}_p^\times)^p$ is generated by the class of $1+p$ for odd $p$, in existential form. It is used to obtain the corresponding normal form for elements of $\mathbb{Q}_p^\times$ modulo $p$-th powers, in [`Padic.exists_eq_prime_pow_mul_one_add_prime_pow_mul_pow`](thm.html#Padic.exists_eq_prime_pow_mul_one_add_prime_pow_mul_pow).
-- source:
